IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `lpul-dates`

-- Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria), Part 4.3 (legal costs) and Part 5.3 Division 3 (costs disputes), with the
-- Victorian Act's s 99 (costs disputes at VCAT) -- the questions of the CLIENT who is charged legal costs.
--
-- SEAT: the person a law practice charges for legal services: its client (for example a person bringing an injury or
-- compensation claim on a "no win, no fee" conditional costs agreement), or a third party payer who must pay the
-- client's costs. "Must my lawyer tell me what this will cost?", "Is my no-win-no-fee agreement valid?", "What is the
-- most uplift fee I can be charged?", "Can I still ask for an itemised bill?", "Can my lawyer sue me yet?", "Can I have
-- the bill assessed, and by when?", "By when must I complain about the bill?", "Can I take the dispute to VCAT?". The law
-- practice is the other party the text names; the Victorian Legal Services Board and Commissioner, the costs assessor,
-- the Costs Court, the Supreme Court and VCAT get no seat: their satisfactions and determinations are asked as facts.
--
-- Every exported question takes plain facts (yes/no, numbers, dates, a list of dates, a choice from a list). The duties
-- with deadlines (ss 177, 181(4), 187(2), (3)) are DEONTIC rules run inside; no question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT.
--
-- Amounts the Law marks "(indexed)" (ss 291-293, Vic Act s 99) are the Law's own figures: no indexed figure is
-- deposited (NOTES.md). The thresholds of s 174(4), (5) are Schedule 4 clause 18(3), (4): $750 and $3000.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Legal
-- Profession Uniform Law Application Act 2014 (Vic), Version No. 018 (11 October 2023), whose Schedule 1 is the Legal
-- Profession Uniform Law, not the authorised version. (c) State of Victoria; published with the authority of the
-- Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No part may be reproduced except in accordance with the Copyright Act
-- 1968 (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Legal costs under the Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria): the client's questions`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Who Part 4.3 protects (s 170, Sch 4 cl 18)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 170(2) "a commercial or government client is a client of a law practice where the client is" one of (a) to (h).
-- The Corporations Act entities of (b)(i)-(v) are one choice: the person says whether they are one (a borrowed term).
DECLARE `Kind of client` IS ONE OF
    `an individual, or another client not listed in s 170(2)`
    `a law practice`
    `a Corporations Act entity listed in s 170(2)(b): public company, large proprietary company, foreign company, their listed subsidiaries, registered Australian body, liquidator, administrator, receiver, financial services licensee or joint-venture proprietary company`
    `an unincorporated joint venture group whose members not entitled to disclosure waived it (s 170(2)(c))`
    `a professional services partnership of more than 20 members, or of large proprietary company size`
    `a body or person incorporated outside Australia`
    `a person who agreed to pay costs on a basis resulting from a tender process`
    `a government authority in Australia or in a foreign country`
    `a person, or a person of a class, specified in the Uniform Rules`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of client`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 170(2) -- a commercial or government client` `the kind` IF
    NOT (`the kind` EQUALS `an individual, or another client not listed in s 170(2)`)

-- Sch 4 cl 18(1)(a) "Part 4.3 of this Law applies to a matter if the client first instructs the law practice on or
-- after the commencement day"; in Victoria Part 4.3 commenced on 1 July 2015 (endnote 2: Chapters 2-7 on 1 July 2015).
`the commencement day for Part 4.3 in Victoria` MEANS `the calendar date` 1 7 2015

-- s 170(1): "This Part does not apply to (a) a commercial or government client; or (b) a third party payer who would be a
-- commercial or government client if the third party payer were a client" -- the same kind input serves both.
-- JUST FALSE where the text says no (s 170(1) flat negative; cl 18(1)(a) "if"); JUST TRUE otherwise. cl 18(2) (a second
-- practice retained after commencement in a matter begun before it) is out of scope.
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of client`
      `the day first instructed` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 170, Sch 4 cl 18 -- Part 4.3 applies to me` `the kind` `the day first instructed` MEANS
    IF `the day` `the day first instructed` `is before` `the commencement day for Part 4.3 in Victoria`
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE IF `s 170(2) -- a commercial or government client` `the kind`
         THEN JUST FALSE
         ELSE JUST TRUE

GIVEN `the answer` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `yes` `the answer` IF
    CONSIDER `the answer`
    WHEN JUST b THEN b
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE

@export Does Part 4.3 of the Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) on legal costs (disclosure, costs agreements, bills, costs assessment) protect me as a client of a law practice, or as a third party payer of a client's legal costs (s 170, Sch 4 cl 18)? It does not protect a commercial or government client (though the rules on conditional costs agreements, uplift fees and contingency fees in ss 181(1), (7), (8), 182, 183 and 185(3)-(5) still apply to them), nor a matter in which the client first instructed the law practice before 1 July 2015.
GIVEN `kind of client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client you are (or, if you are a third party payer, would be if you were the client): an individual or other client not listed; a law practice; a listed Corporations Act entity (public company, large proprietary company, foreign company, their listed subsidiaries, registered Australian body, liquidator, administrator, receiver, financial services licensee, joint-venture proprietary company); an unincorporated joint venture group that waived disclosure; a professional partnership of more than 20 members or of large company size; a body incorporated outside Australia; a person who agreed costs through a tender; a government authority; or a person the Uniform Rules specify
      `day first instructed` IS A DATE @desc The day the client first instructed the law practice in this matter (ISO date, e.g. 2026-03-15)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does Part 4.3 protect me` MEANS
    `s 170, Sch 4 cl 18 -- Part 4.3 applies to me` `kind of client` `day first instructed`

@export Can I require my law practice to negotiate a costs agreement with me (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 179)? A client protected by Part 4.3 has the right to require and to have a negotiated costs agreement.
GIVEN `kind of client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client you are: an individual or other client not listed in s 170(2); or one of the commercial or government kinds (law practice, listed Corporations Act entity, joint venture group, large partnership, foreign-incorporated body, tender, government authority, Uniform Rules class)
      `day first instructed` IS A DATE @desc The day you first instructed the law practice in this matter (ISO date)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I require a negotiated costs agreement` MEANS
    `s 170, Sch 4 cl 18 -- Part 4.3 applies to me` `kind of client` `day first instructed`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Costs disclosure (ss 174-178)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 174(4): the lower threshold, Sch 4 cl 18(3) "$750"; s 174(5): the higher threshold, Sch 4 cl 18(4) "$3000". Both
-- compare "total legal costs in the matter (excluding GST and disbursements)" that "are not likely to exceed" the amount.
`the lower threshold` MEANS 750
`the higher threshold` MEANS 3000

DECLARE `The disclosure I must be given at the start` IS ONE OF
    `none is required: the total legal costs are not likely to exceed the lower threshold of $750, though the law practice may give me the uniform standard disclosure form (s 174(4))`
    `the uniform standard disclosure form, or full written disclosure: the total legal costs are not likely to exceed the higher threshold of $3000 (s 174(5), (6))`
    `full written disclosure of the basis of the costs, an estimate of the total costs, and my rights, when or as soon as practicable after I first instruct (s 174(1)(a), (2)(a), (6))`
    `none from this law practice: it was retained for me by another law practice, which must disclose this practice's costs to me as well as its own (s 175)`
    `not covered: Part 4.3 does not protect me (s 170, Sch 4 cl 18)`

GIVEN `the likely total` IS A NUMBER
      `retained by another practice that still acts` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `The disclosure I must be given at the start`
`s 174(1), (4), (5), 175 -- the disclosure at the start` `the likely total` `retained by another practice that still acts` MEANS
    IF `retained by another practice that still acts`
    THEN `none from this law practice: it was retained for me by another law practice, which must disclose this practice's costs to me as well as its own (s 175)`
    ELSE IF `the likely total` AT MOST `the lower threshold`
         THEN `none is required: the total legal costs are not likely to exceed the lower threshold of $750, though the law practice may give me the uniform standard disclosure form (s 174(4))`
         ELSE IF `the likely total` AT MOST `the higher threshold`
              THEN `the uniform standard disclosure form, or full written disclosure: the total legal costs are not likely to exceed the higher threshold of $3000 (s 174(5), (6))`
              ELSE `full written disclosure of the basis of the costs, an estimate of the total costs, and my rights, when or as soon as practicable after I first instruct (s 174(1)(a), (2)(a), (6))`

@export What costs disclosure must my law practice give me when I first instruct it (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) ss 174, 175)? Above $3000 of likely legal costs (excluding GST and disbursements) you must be given full written disclosure; up to $3000 the uniform standard disclosure form will do; up to $750 none is required. A law practice briefed for you by your own law practice (for example a barrister) discloses through that practice.
GIVEN `kind of client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client you are: an individual or other client not listed in s 170(2); or one of the commercial or government kinds
      `day first instructed` IS A DATE @desc The day you first instructed the law practice in this matter (ISO date)
      `likely total legal costs` IS A NUMBER @desc The total legal costs in the matter that were likely when you first instructed, in dollars, excluding GST and disbursements (out-of-pocket expenses such as court fees)
      `retained for me by another law practice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this law practice was retained on your behalf by another law practice (for example a barrister briefed by your solicitor) that still acts for you
GIVETH A `The disclosure I must be given at the start`
`What disclosure must I be given at the start` MEANS
    IF `yes` (`s 170, Sch 4 cl 18 -- Part 4.3 applies to me` `kind of client` `day first instructed`)
    THEN `s 174(1), (4), (5), 175 -- the disclosure at the start` `likely total legal costs` `retained for me by another law practice`
    ELSE `not covered: Part 4.3 does not protect me (s 170, Sch 4 cl 18)`

-- s 174(1)(a), (2)(a), (3), (6): what full disclosure must contain. Each limb is a "must": the disclosure meets the
-- section only if every limb is met. The law practice's satisfying itself (s 174(3)) is asked as a fact.
-- Finding 1: s 174(2)(a)(iv) names "the designated local regulatory authority", which for Chapter 4 is the Victorian
-- Legal Services Board (Vic Act s 10, Table 1 item 6), not the Commissioner who deals with costs disputes (item 7).
GIVEN `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `when or soon after instructions` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the basis of calculation` IS A BOOLEAN
      `an estimate of the total` IS A BOOLEAN
      `right to negotiate a costs agreement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `right to negotiate the billing method` IS A BOOLEAN
      `right to a bill and an itemised bill` IS A BOOLEAN
      `right to the regulator's help` IS A BOOLEAN
      `reasonable steps on understanding and consent` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 174(1)(a), (2)(a), (3), (6) -- the full disclosure meets the section` `in writing` `when or soon after instructions` `the basis of calculation` `an estimate of the total` `right to negotiate a costs agreement` `right to negotiate the billing method` `right to a bill and an itemised bill` `right to the regulator's help` `reasonable steps on understanding and consent` IF
        `in writing`
    AND `when or soon after instructions`
    AND `the basis of calculation`
    AND `an estimate of the total`
    AND `right to negotiate a costs agreement`
    AND `right to negotiate the billing method`
    AND `right to a bill and an itemised bill`
    AND `right to the regulator's help`
    AND `reasonable steps on understanding and consent`

@export Did the full costs disclosure my law practice gave me meet the Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 174(1)(a), (2)(a), (3) and (6)? It must be in writing, given when or as soon as practicable after you first instructed, and state how the costs will be calculated, an estimate of the total costs, and your rights to negotiate a costs agreement and the billing method, to receive a bill and request an itemised bill, and to seek the regulator's help in a costs dispute; and the practice must take all reasonable steps to satisfy itself that you understood and consented.
GIVEN `in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the disclosure was in writing
      `given when or soon after I instructed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was given when, or as soon as practicable after, you first gave instructions in the matter
      `states how costs are calculated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it disclosed the basis on which legal costs will be calculated (for example hourly rates or a fixed fee)
      `estimates the total` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it gave an estimate of the total legal costs
      `right to negotiate a costs agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it told you of your right to negotiate a costs agreement with the law practice
      `right to negotiate billing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it told you of your right to negotiate the billing method (for example by timing or by task)
      `right to bills` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it told you of your right to receive a bill and to request an itemised bill after receiving a bill that is not, or only partly, itemised
      `right to regulator's help` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it told you of your right to seek the help of the regulator (the Victorian Legal Services Board) if there is a dispute about legal costs
      `practice took reasonable steps` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice took all reasonable steps to satisfy itself that you understood and consented to the proposed course of action and the proposed costs
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Did my full disclosure meet s 174` MEANS
    `s 174(1)(a), (2)(a), (3), (6) -- the full disclosure meets the section` `in writing` `given when or soon after I instructed` `states how costs are calculated` `estimates the total` `right to negotiate a costs agreement` `right to negotiate billing` `right to bills` `right to regulator's help` `practice took reasonable steps`

DECLARE `What I was given at the start` IS ONE OF
    `no disclosure, because the costs were not likely to exceed $750`
    `the uniform standard disclosure form`
    `full disclosure under s 174(1)`

DECLARE `Further disclosure I must be given` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I must be told in writing that the costs are now likely to exceed $750, and be given the uniform standard disclosure form or full disclosure (s 174(7))`
    `yes: I must be told in writing that the costs are now likely to exceed $750, and be given full disclosure (s 174(7))`
    `yes: I must be told in writing that the costs are now likely to exceed $3000, and be given full disclosure (s 174(8))`
    `yes: I must be told in writing of the significant change, with enough about its impact on my costs to decide how to go on (s 174(1)(b), (2)(b), (6))`
    `no: s 174 requires no further disclosure on these facts`
    `not covered: Part 4.3 does not protect me (s 170, Sch 4 cl 18)`

-- s 174(7): nothing given because below $750; costs now likely above $750: inform in writing and make the disclosure of
-- (1) "or (if applicable) subsection (5)" -- the form is applicable while the likely total stays at or below $3000.
-- s 174(8): the form given because below $3000; now likely above $3000: inform and make full disclosure.
-- s 174(1)(b): after full disclosure, a significant change to anything disclosed.
GIVEN `what I was given` IS A `What I was given at the start`
      `the likely total now` IS A NUMBER
      `a significant change` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Further disclosure I must be given`
`s 174(1)(b), (7), (8) -- the further disclosure` `what I was given` `the likely total now` `a significant change` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what I was given`
    WHEN `no disclosure, because the costs were not likely to exceed $750` THEN
        IF `the likely total now` AT MOST `the lower threshold`
        THEN `no: s 174 requires no further disclosure on these facts`
        ELSE IF `the likely total now` AT MOST `the higher threshold`
             THEN `yes: I must be told in writing that the costs are now likely to exceed $750, and be given the uniform standard disclosure form or full disclosure (s 174(7))`
             ELSE `yes: I must be told in writing that the costs are now likely to exceed $750, and be given full disclosure (s 174(7))`
    WHEN `the uniform standard disclosure form` THEN
        IF `the likely total now` AT MOST `the higher threshold`
        THEN `no: s 174 requires no further disclosure on these facts`
        ELSE `yes: I must be told in writing that the costs are now likely to exceed $3000, and be given full disclosure (s 174(8))`
    WHEN `full disclosure under s 174(1)` THEN
        IF `a significant change`
        THEN `yes: I must be told in writing of the significant change, with enough about its impact on my costs to decide how to go on (s 174(1)(b), (2)(b), (6))`
        ELSE `no: s 174 requires no further disclosure on these facts`

@export Must my law practice now give me further costs disclosure (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 174(1)(b), (7), (8))? If you were given nothing because costs were likely to be $750 or less, or only the standard form because they were likely to be $3000 or less, and the likely total has now gone over that threshold, you must be told in writing and given the disclosure the new total needs; after full disclosure, you must be told of any significant change. The duty arises when or as soon as practicable after the practice becomes aware, or ought reasonably to become aware, of the change.
GIVEN `kind of client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client you are: an individual or other client not listed in s 170(2); or one of the commercial or government kinds
      `day first instructed` IS A DATE @desc The day you first instructed the law practice in this matter (ISO date)
      `what I was given at the start` IS A `What I was given at the start` @desc What costs disclosure you were given at the start: none, because the costs were not likely to exceed $750; the uniform standard disclosure form; or full disclosure
      `likely total now` IS A NUMBER @desc The total legal costs now likely in the matter, in dollars, excluding GST and disbursements
      `significant change` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there has been a significant change to anything in the full disclosure you were given (for example to the estimate or the basis of charging)
GIVETH A `Further disclosure I must be given`
`Must I be given further disclosure` MEANS
    IF `yes` (`s 170, Sch 4 cl 18 -- Part 4.3 applies to me` `kind of client` `day first instructed`)
    THEN `s 174(1)(b), (7), (8) -- the further disclosure` `what I was given at the start` `likely total now` `significant change`
    ELSE `not covered: Part 4.3 does not protect me (s 170, Sch 4 cl 18)`

-- s 177(1) "If a law practice negotiates the settlement of a litigious matter on behalf of a client, the law practice must
-- disclose to the client, before the settlement is executed" (a) and (b). (2) a practice retained by another practice
-- need not, if the other practice makes the disclosure before execution.
-- The duty as a rule with a deadline, counted in half-days from the earlier of the two days (day 0): the settlement is
-- executed in the second half of its day; a disclosure on that day "before the settlement is executed" is in its first
-- half; any other disclosure at the start of its day. The practice MUST disclose WITHIN the first half of the day of
-- execution, LEST BREACH. Fork F3: a disclosure on the day of execution counts only if made before execution.
DECLARE `Party` IS ONE OF `I`, `my law practice`
DECLARE `Act done` IS ONE OF
    `disclose the settlement costs estimates`
    `end the conditional costs agreement by written notice`
    `request an itemised bill`
    `give the itemised bill`

GIVEN `the half-days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 177 -- disclose before the settlement is executed` `the half-days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `my law practice`
    MUST `disclose the settlement costs estimates`
    WITHIN `the half-days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `my law practice` BECAUSE "the estimates of the costs payable on settlement and of the contributions expected were not disclosed before the settlement was executed (s 177(1))"

-- The helper that runs a rule over what happened (as in the Victims of Crime and County Court Act encodings). On the
-- stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not BREACH. It takes a DEONTIC value, so it is never
-- exported.
GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
      `who` IS A `Party`
      `the act` IS A `Act done`
      `the time it was done` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `done in time under` `the rule` `by` `who` `doing` `the act` `at` `the time it was done` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the time it was done`), (EVENT `who` `the act` `the time it was done`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

DECLARE `Settlement disclosure` IS ONE OF
    `s 177 does not apply: my law practice is not negotiating the settlement of a litigious matter for me`
    `s 177 does not apply to this law practice: the law practice that retained it for me made the disclosure before the settlement was executed (s 177(2))`
    `met: both estimates were disclosed to me before the settlement was executed (s 177(1))`
    `not met: both estimates were not disclosed to me before the settlement was executed, a contravention of the disclosure obligations (ss 177(1), 178)`

GIVEN `litigious` IS A BOOLEAN
      `negotiating the settlement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the retaining practice disclosed in time` IS A BOOLEAN
      `both estimates` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day disclosed` IS A DATE
      `the day executed` IS A DATE
      `before execution on that day` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Settlement disclosure`
`s 177 -- the settlement disclosure` `litigious` `negotiating the settlement` `the retaining practice disclosed in time` `both estimates` `the day disclosed` `the day executed` `before execution on that day` MEANS
    IF NOT (`litigious` AND `negotiating the settlement`)
    THEN `s 177 does not apply: my law practice is not negotiating the settlement of a litigious matter for me`
    ELSE IF `the retaining practice disclosed in time`
         THEN `s 177 does not apply to this law practice: the law practice that retained it for me made the disclosure before the settlement was executed (s 177(2))`
         ELSE IF `both estimates` AND `done in time under` (`s 177 -- disclose before the settlement is executed` `the deadline`) `by` `my law practice` `doing` `disclose the settlement costs estimates` `at` `the time disclosed`
              THEN `met: both estimates were disclosed to me before the settlement was executed (s 177(1))`
              ELSE `not met: both estimates were not disclosed to me before the settlement was executed, a contravention of the disclosure obligations (ss 177(1), 178)`
    WHERE
        `day 0` MEANS `the earlier of` `the day disclosed` `and` `the day executed`
        `the deadline` MEANS 2 TIMES (`the days from` `day 0` `to` `the day executed`)
        `the time disclosed` MEANS
            IF DATE_SERIAL `the day disclosed` EQUALS DATE_SERIAL `the day executed`
            THEN IF `before execution on that day`
                 THEN `the deadline`
                 ELSE `the deadline` PLUS 2
            ELSE 2 TIMES (`the days from` `day 0` `to` `the day disclosed`)

@export Before I settle my court or tribunal case, did my law practice give me in time the costs estimates the Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 177 requires? A law practice negotiating the settlement of a litigious matter for you must, before the settlement is executed, disclose a reasonable estimate of the legal costs you will pay if it settles (including any other party's costs you are to pay) and a reasonable estimate of any contributions to those costs you are likely to receive from another party.
GIVEN `litigious matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter involves, or is likely to involve, proceedings in a court or tribunal
      `negotiating the settlement for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this law practice is negotiating the settlement on your behalf
      `my other law practice disclosed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this law practice was retained for you by another law practice, and that other practice gave you both estimates before the settlement was executed
      `both estimates given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were given both a reasonable estimate of the legal costs you will pay on settlement (including other parties' costs you are to pay) and a reasonable estimate of contributions likely from another party
      `day estimates given` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given the estimates (ISO date); if never given, the day of execution
      `day settlement executed` IS A DATE @desc The day the settlement was, or is to be, executed (signed) (ISO date)
      `given before signing that day` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the estimates were given on the day of execution, whether they were given before the settlement was executed; otherwise answer no
GIVETH A `Settlement disclosure`
`Was I given the settlement estimates in time` MEANS
    `s 177 -- the settlement disclosure` `litigious matter` `negotiating the settlement for me` `my other law practice disclosed` `both estimates given` `day estimates given` `day settlement executed` `given before signing that day`

-- s 178(1): if a law practice contravenes the disclosure obligations (a) the costs agreement is void; (b) the client or an
-- associated third party payer "is not required to pay the legal costs until they have been assessed or any costs
-- dispute has been determined"; (c) the practice must not commence or maintain proceedings to recover them until then.
-- (2): where disclosure was made to one of client and associated third party payer but not the other, the one disclosed
-- to is still liable and may be sued. (3) and General Rules r 72A disapply (1) and (2) where the regulator, a costs
-- assessor, a court or a tribunal is satisfied of three things -- asked as one fact.
DECLARE `What follows from the failure to disclose` IS ONE OF
    `my costs agreement (if any) is void, and I need not pay the legal costs until they have been assessed or a costs dispute about them has been determined; the law practice cannot sue me for them until then (s 178(1))`
    `nothing under s 178 for me: the disclosure was made to me, and only the other of client and third party payer missed out (s 178(2))`
    `nothing under s 178: it has been found that the law practice took reasonable steps, fixed the failure within 14 days, and the failure made no difference (s 178(3), Uniform Rules r 72A)`
    `nothing under s 178: the law practice did not contravene the disclosure obligations on these facts`
    `not covered: Part 4.3 does not protect me (s 170, Sch 4 cl 18)`

GIVEN `contravened` IS A BOOLEAN
      `disclosed to me but not the other` IS A BOOLEAN
      `r 72A finding` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `What follows from the failure to disclose`
`s 178 -- what follows` `contravened` `disclosed to me but not the other` `r 72A finding` MEANS
    IF NOT `contravened`
    THEN `nothing under s 178: the law practice did not contravene the disclosure obligations on these facts`
    ELSE IF `r 72A finding`
         THEN `nothing under s 178: it has been found that the law practice took reasonable steps, fixed the failure within 14 days, and the failure made no difference (s 178(3), Uniform Rules r 72A)`
         ELSE IF `disclosed to me but not the other`
              THEN `nothing under s 178 for me: the disclosure was made to me, and only the other of client and third party payer missed out (s 178(2))`
              ELSE `my costs agreement (if any) is void, and I need not pay the legal costs until they have been assessed or a costs dispute about them has been determined; the law practice cannot sue me for them until then (s 178(1))`

@export My law practice did not give me the costs disclosure Part 4.3 requires: what follows (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 178)? The costs agreement is void, you need not pay until the costs are assessed or a costs dispute is determined, and the practice cannot sue you until then; unless a regulator, costs assessor, court or tribunal has found the failure was promptly fixed and made no difference (Uniform Rules r 72A).
GIVEN `kind of client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client you are (or would be, if you are a third party payer): an individual or other client not listed in s 170(2); or one of the commercial or government kinds
      `day first instructed` IS A DATE @desc The day the client first instructed the law practice in this matter (ISO date)
      `disclosure obligations broken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice failed to make a costs disclosure Part 4.3 required (at the start, on a change, about another practice it retained, to a third party payer, or before a settlement)
      `I was disclosed to, the other was not` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is both a client and a third party payer who owes the law practice, and the disclosure was made to you but not to the other
      `found fixed and harmless` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the regulator, a costs assessor, a court or a tribunal has found that the law practice took reasonable steps to comply, fixed the failure within 14 days of becoming aware of it, and the failure was not substantial and would not have changed your decisions; answer no if not so found
GIVETH A `What follows from the failure to disclose`
`What follows from the failure to disclose to me` MEANS
    IF `yes` (`s 170, Sch 4 cl 18 -- Part 4.3 applies to me` `kind of client` `day first instructed`)
    THEN `s 178 -- what follows` `disclosure obligations broken` `I was disclosed to, the other was not` `found fixed and harmless`
    ELSE `not covered: Part 4.3 does not protect me (s 170, Sch 4 cl 18)`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Conditional ("no win, no fee") costs agreements, uplift fees and contingency fees (ss 180-185)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

DECLARE `Kind of matter` IS ONE OF
    `criminal proceedings`
    `proceedings under the Family Law Act 1975 (Cth)`
    `proceedings under legislation the Uniform Rules specify for s 181`
    `another matter (for example a personal injury or compensation claim)`

DECLARE `Whether my conditional costs agreement is valid` IS ONE OF
    `valid on these facts: none of the defects in ss 178, 180-183 applies`
    `void: a conditional costs agreement cannot be made in this kind of matter (ss 181(7), 185(1))`
    `void: it is not in writing and in plain language, or does not set out what counts as a successful outcome (ss 181(2), 185(1))`
    `void: it is not signed by me, or does not state that I was told of my right to independent legal advice (ss 181(3), 185(1))`
    `void: its cooling-off period is shorter than 5 clear business days (ss 181(4), 185(1))`
    `void: it purports to exclude the costs from costs assessment (ss 180(4), 185(1))`
    `void: it provides for an uplift fee in a litigious matter without the law practice reasonably believing success reasonably likely (ss 182(2)(a), 185(1)); the uplift fee cannot be recovered (s 185(3))`
    `void: its uplift fee is more than 25% of the legal costs otherwise payable in a litigious matter (ss 182(2)(b), 185(1)); the uplift fee cannot be recovered (s 185(3))`
    `void: it does not state the basis of the uplift fee and an estimate, or a range of estimates with the major variables (ss 182(3), 185(1)); the uplift fee cannot be recovered (s 185(3))`
    `void: it is a prohibited contingency fee, calculated by reference to the amount recovered (ss 183, 185(1)); the law practice can recover nothing for its services in the matter (s 185(4))`
    `void: the law practice broke its costs disclosure obligations (s 178(1)(a))`
    `not covered: Part 4.3 does not protect me (s 170); ss 181(7), 182, 183 and 185(3)-(5) still apply to a commercial or government client`

-- ss 180-183 and 185(1): the checks in the order of the sections that apply to the agreement as a whole, then the uplift
-- fee, then the contingency fee, then s 178. The first defect found is the answer; each is enough on its own (fork F4).
-- s 183(1) "calculated by reference to the amount of any award or settlement or the value of any property that may be
-- recovered"; (2) "does not apply to the extent that the costs agreement adopts an applicable fixed costs legislative
-- provision".
GIVEN `the matter` IS A `Kind of matter`
      `written and plain` IS A BOOLEAN
      `success defined` IS A BOOLEAN
      `signed by me` IS A BOOLEAN
      `advice statement` IS A BOOLEAN
      `cooling-off business days` IS A NUMBER
      `excludes assessment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `uplift` IS A BOOLEAN
      `litigious` IS A BOOLEAN
      `reasonable belief in success` IS A BOOLEAN
      `uplift percent` IS A NUMBER
      `uplift basis and estimate` IS A BOOLEAN
      `by reference to the amount recovered` IS A BOOLEAN
      `adopts a fixed costs provision` IS A BOOLEAN
      `disclosure broken` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Whether my conditional costs agreement is valid`
`ss 178, 180-185 -- the conditional costs agreement` `the matter` `written and plain` `success defined` `signed by me` `advice statement` `cooling-off business days` `excludes assessment` `uplift` `litigious` `reasonable belief in success` `uplift percent` `uplift basis and estimate` `by reference to the amount recovered` `adopts a fixed costs provision` `disclosure broken` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT (`the matter` EQUALS `another matter (for example a personal injury or compensation claim)`)
           THEN `void: a conditional costs agreement cannot be made in this kind of matter (ss 181(7), 185(1))`
           IF NOT (`written and plain` AND `success defined`)
           THEN `void: it is not in writing and in plain language, or does not set out what counts as a successful outcome (ss 181(2), 185(1))`
           IF NOT (`signed by me` AND `advice statement`)
           THEN `void: it is not signed by me, or does not state that I was told of my right to independent legal advice (ss 181(3), 185(1))`
           IF `cooling-off business days` LESS THAN 5
           THEN `void: its cooling-off period is shorter than 5 clear business days (ss 181(4), 185(1))`
           IF `excludes assessment`
           THEN `void: it purports to exclude the costs from costs assessment (ss 180(4), 185(1))`
           IF `uplift` AND `litigious` AND (NOT `reasonable belief in success`)
           THEN `void: it provides for an uplift fee in a litigious matter without the law practice reasonably believing success reasonably likely (ss 182(2)(a), 185(1)); the uplift fee cannot be recovered (s 185(3))`
           IF `uplift` AND `litigious` AND `uplift percent` GREATER THAN 25
           THEN `void: its uplift fee is more than 25% of the legal costs otherwise payable in a litigious matter (ss 182(2)(b), 185(1)); the uplift fee cannot be recovered (s 185(3))`
           IF `uplift` AND (NOT `uplift basis and estimate`)
           THEN `void: it does not state the basis of the uplift fee and an estimate, or a range of estimates with the major variables (ss 182(3), 185(1)); the uplift fee cannot be recovered (s 185(3))`
           IF `by reference to the amount recovered` AND (NOT `adopts a fixed costs provision`)
           THEN `void: it is a prohibited contingency fee, calculated by reference to the amount recovered (ss 183, 185(1)); the law practice can recover nothing for its services in the matter (s 185(4))`
           IF `disclosure broken`
           THEN `void: the law practice broke its costs disclosure obligations (s 178(1)(a))`
           OTHERWISE `valid on these facts: none of the defects in ss 178, 180-183 applies`

@export Is my conditional ("no win, no fee") costs agreement with my law practice valid, or void (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) ss 178, 180-185)? It must be in writing and plain language, say what a successful outcome is, be signed by you, state that you were told of your right to independent legal advice, and give you a cooling-off period of at least 5 clear business days; it cannot be used in criminal or family law proceedings; an uplift fee in a court case needs a reasonable belief in success and cannot exceed 25%; a fee taken as a share of the amount you recover is prohibited.
GIVEN `kind of client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client you are: an individual or other client not listed in s 170(2); or one of the commercial or government kinds
      `day first instructed` IS A DATE @desc The day you first instructed the law practice in this matter (ISO date)
      `kind of matter` IS A `Kind of matter` @desc What the matter is: criminal proceedings; proceedings under the Family Law Act 1975; proceedings under legislation the Uniform Rules specify; or another matter (such as an injury or compensation claim)
      `written in plain language` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement is in writing and in plain language
      `says what success is` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it sets out the circumstances that count as a successful outcome of the matter
      `signed by me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you signed it
      `states my right to independent advice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it states that you were told of your right to seek independent legal advice before entering into it
      `cooling-off period in business days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many clear business days the agreement gives you to end it by written notice (the cooling-off period); 0 if none
      `excludes costs assessment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement says the costs cannot be assessed
      `has an uplift fee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it provides for an uplift fee: extra legal costs (excluding disbursements) payable if the matter succeeds
      `litigious matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter involves, or is likely to involve, proceedings in a court or tribunal
      `practice believed success reasonably likely` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice had a reasonable belief that a successful outcome was reasonably likely when the uplift fee was agreed; answer yes if there is no uplift fee
      `uplift fee percent` IS A NUMBER @desc The uplift fee as a percentage of the legal costs (excluding disbursements) otherwise payable, e.g. 25 for 25%; 0 if none
      `states uplift basis and estimate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement states how the uplift fee is calculated and gives an estimate of it (or, if that is not reasonably practical, a range of estimates and the major variables); answer yes if there is no uplift fee
      `fee is a share of what I recover` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any amount payable to the law practice is calculated by reference to the amount of an award or settlement, or the value of property, that may be recovered (a contingency fee)
      `follows a fixed costs scale` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether that calculation only adopts a fixed costs provision made by legislation or the Uniform Rules; answer no if unsure
      `disclosure obligations broken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice failed to make a costs disclosure Part 4.3 required of it
GIVETH A `Whether my conditional costs agreement is valid`
`Is my conditional costs agreement valid` MEANS
    IF `yes` (`s 170, Sch 4 cl 18 -- Part 4.3 applies to me` `kind of client` `day first instructed`)
    THEN `ss 178, 180-185 -- the conditional costs agreement` `kind of matter` `written in plain language` `says what success is` `signed by me` `states my right to independent advice` `cooling-off period in business days` `excludes costs assessment` `has an uplift fee` `litigious matter` `practice believed success reasonably likely` `uplift fee percent` `states uplift basis and estimate` `fee is a share of what I recover` `follows a fixed costs scale` `disclosure obligations broken`
    ELSE `not covered: Part 4.3 does not protect me (s 170); ss 181(7), 182, 183 and 185(3)-(5) still apply to a commercial or government client`

-- s 182(2)(b) "the uplift fee must not exceed 25% of the legal costs (excluding disbursements) otherwise payable" -- for a
-- litigious matter only. Outside a litigious matter the Law sets no cap: NOTHING (a scope limit, ruling (c)).
-- s 182 applies to commercial and government clients too (s 170(1)), so no s 170 test here.
GIVEN `litigious` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the costs otherwise payable` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 182(2)(b) -- the most uplift fee` `litigious` `the costs otherwise payable` MEANS
    IF `litigious`
    THEN JUST (`the costs otherwise payable` TIMES 25 DIVIDED BY 100)
    ELSE NOTHING

@export What is the most uplift fee my conditional ("no win, no fee") costs agreement can make me pay if I win my court or tribunal case (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 182(2)(b)), in dollars? In a litigious matter the uplift fee cannot exceed 25% of the legal costs, excluding disbursements, otherwise payable. No answer is given for a matter that does not involve court or tribunal proceedings: the Law sets no cap there.
GIVEN `litigious matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the matter involves, or is likely to involve, proceedings in a court or tribunal
      `legal costs otherwise payable` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs you would pay without the uplift fee, in dollars, excluding disbursements (out-of-pocket expenses such as court fees and expert reports)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the most uplift fee` MEANS
    `s 182(2)(b) -- the most uplift fee` `litigious matter` `legal costs otherwise payable`

-- s 181(4) "a cooling-off period of not less than 5 clear business days during which the client, by written notice, may
-- terminate the agreement". The period runs from the day after the agreement is made (that day not counted, "clear":
-- fork F2) to the end of the last business day of the period. Run as a rule: I DO end it WITHIN the days to that last
-- day, LEST the period has ended (the "breach" is the period running out, not a wrong).
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 181(4) -- end within the cooling-off period` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `end the conditional costs agreement by written notice`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the cooling-off period ended before the written notice was given (s 181(4)); s 181(5) does not apply"

GIVEN `the day made` IS A DATE
      `the period` IS A NUMBER
      `the holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 181(4) -- the last day of the cooling-off period` `the day made` `the period` `the holidays` MEANS
    `the business day` `the period` `after` `the day made` `given the holidays` `the holidays`

GIVEN `the day made` IS A DATE
      `the period` IS A NUMBER
      `the holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE
      `the day of notice` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 181(4) -- ended within the cooling-off period` `the day made` `the period` `the holidays` `the day of notice` IF
        `the day` `the day made` `is on or before` `the day of notice`
    AND `done in time under` (`s 181(4) -- end within the cooling-off period` (`the days from` `the day made` `to` (`s 181(4) -- the last day of the cooling-off period` `the day made` `the period` `the holidays`))) `by` `I` `doing` `end the conditional costs agreement by written notice` `at` (`the days from` `the day made` `to` `the day of notice`)

@export What is the last day on which I can end my conditional ("no win, no fee") costs agreement by written notice in its cooling-off period (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 181(4))? The agreement must give you at least 5 clear business days, not counting the day it was made, weekends or public holidays. No answer is given if the agreement gives fewer than 5: it is then void.
GIVEN `day agreement made` IS A DATE @desc The day the conditional costs agreement was made (ISO date)
      `cooling-off period in business days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many clear business days of cooling-off the agreement gives you
      `public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc The public, special and bank holidays in the place where you are to give the notice, from the day the agreement was made onwards (ISO dates); an empty list if none
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`What is the last day of my cooling-off period` MEANS
    IF `cooling-off period in business days` LESS THAN 5
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST (`s 181(4) -- the last day of the cooling-off period` `day agreement made` `cooling-off period in business days` `public holidays`)

-- s 181(5) "If a client terminates a conditional costs agreement within the cooling-off period, the law practice (a) may
-- recover only those legal costs in respect of legal services performed for the client before that termination that
-- were performed on the instructions of the client and with the client's knowledge that the legal services would be
-- performed during that period; and (b) in particular, may not recover any uplift fee." An "if": when the notice was not
-- within the period, s 181(5) gives no limit and the answer is NOTHING (the agreement governs).
GIVEN `in time` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the costs on my instructions and knowledge` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 181(5) -- what the law practice may recover after cooling-off` `in time` `the costs on my instructions and knowledge` MEANS
    IF `in time`
    THEN JUST `the costs on my instructions and knowledge`
    ELSE NOTHING

@export I ended my conditional ("no win, no fee") costs agreement by written notice: if that was in the cooling-off period, what is the most my law practice can recover from me (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 181(4), (5)), in dollars? Only the legal costs for work done before you ended it, on your instructions and with your knowledge that it would be done in the cooling-off period; never any uplift fee. No answer is given if the notice came after the cooling-off period, or the agreement gave fewer than 5 business days.
GIVEN `day agreement made` IS A DATE @desc The day the conditional costs agreement was made (ISO date)
      `cooling-off period in business days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many clear business days of cooling-off the agreement gives you
      `public holidays` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc The public, special and bank holidays in the place where you gave the notice, from the day the agreement was made onwards (ISO dates); an empty list if none
      `day I gave written notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave the law practice written notice ending the agreement (ISO date)
      `costs for work on my instructions` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs, in dollars, for work done before you ended the agreement that was done on your instructions and with your knowledge that it would be done in the cooling-off period (excluding any uplift fee)
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What can my lawyer recover after I ended the agreement` MEANS
    IF `cooling-off period in business days` LESS THAN 5
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE `s 181(5) -- what the law practice may recover after cooling-off` (`s 181(4) -- ended within the cooling-off period` `day agreement made` `cooling-off period in business days` `public holidays` `day I gave written notice`) `costs for work on my instructions`

-- s 185(2)-(5): repayment when a costs agreement is void. (4) a contingency fee (s 183): nothing is recoverable for the
-- services and everything received for them must be repaid. (3) an uplift fee contravention (s 182): the uplift fee is not
-- recoverable and the amount received for it must be repaid. (2) any other contravention of Division 4: no more than
-- would have been recoverable had the agreement not been void, and any excess must be repaid. (5) recoverable as a debt.
-- A s 178 voidness (disclosure) is not under s 185: the costs are assessed instead (NOTHING here). Fork F5: for s 182 the
-- answer is the uplift received; s 185(2) may add an excess on the rest of the costs.
DECLARE `Why my costs agreement is void` IS ONE OF
    `a prohibited contingency fee (s 183)`
    `an uplift fee in contravention of s 182`
    `another contravention of Division 4 (ss 180, 181)`
    `the law practice broke its disclosure obligations (s 178)`

GIVEN `why` IS A `Why my costs agreement is void`
      `paid for the services` IS A NUMBER
      `paid as uplift` IS A NUMBER
      `recoverable had it not been void` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 185(2)-(4) -- what must be repaid` `why` `paid for the services` `paid as uplift` `recoverable had it not been void` MEANS
    CONSIDER `why`
    WHEN `a prohibited contingency fee (s 183)` THEN JUST `paid for the services`
    WHEN `an uplift fee in contravention of s 182` THEN JUST `paid as uplift`
    WHEN `another contravention of Division 4 (ss 180, 181)` THEN
        IF `paid for the services` GREATER THAN `recoverable had it not been void`
        THEN JUST (`paid for the services` MINUS `recoverable had it not been void`)
        ELSE JUST 0
    WHEN `the law practice broke its disclosure obligations (s 178)` THEN NOTHING

@export My costs agreement is void: how much must my law practice repay me (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 185(2)-(5)), in dollars? For a prohibited contingency fee, everything you paid for its services in the matter; for an uplift fee agreed in breach of s 182, the uplift fee you paid; for another breach of the costs agreement rules, what you paid beyond what it could have recovered had the agreement not been void. You can recover an unpaid repayment as a debt in court. No answer is given where the agreement is void for failure to disclose: the costs are then assessed (s 178).
GIVEN `why it is void` IS A `Why my costs agreement is void` @desc Why the agreement is void: a prohibited contingency fee (a share of what is recovered); an uplift fee agreed in breach of s 182; another breach of the costs agreement rules (ss 180, 181); or the law practice's failure to disclose costs (s 178)
      `paid for the services` IS A NUMBER @desc How much you have paid the law practice for its legal services in the matter, in dollars (including any uplift fee)
      `paid as uplift fee` IS A NUMBER @desc How much of that you paid as the uplift fee, in dollars; 0 if none
      `recoverable without the void agreement` IS A NUMBER @desc How much the law practice would have been entitled to recover had the agreement not been void (for example as fixed on a costs assessment), in dollars; only used for another breach of the costs agreement rules
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`How much must my lawyer repay me` MEANS
    `s 185(2)-(4) -- what must be repaid` `why it is void` `paid for the services` `paid as uplift fee` `recoverable without the void agreement`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Bills, progress reports and interest (ss 187-195)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 187(1) "If a bill is given ... in the form of a lump sum bill, any person who is entitled to apply for an assessment
-- ... may request ... an itemised bill." (2) "within 30 days after the date on which the legal costs become payable".
-- The last day is the payable day plus 30 (fork F6: "within n days after" a day ends n days later).
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 187(2) -- request within 30 days` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `I`
    DO `request an itemised bill`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `I` BECAUSE "the request was not made within 30 days after the legal costs became payable (s 187(2))"

GIVEN `lump sum` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day payable` IS A DATE
      `the day of request` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 187(1), (2) -- my request for an itemised bill is in time` `lump sum` `the day payable` `the day of request` IF
        `lump sum`
    AND `done in time under` (`s 187(2) -- request within 30 days` 30) `by` `I` `doing` `request an itemised bill` `at` (`the days from` `the day payable` `to` `the day of request`)

@export Can I ask my law practice for an itemised bill instead of its lump sum bill (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 187(1), (2))? Anyone entitled to apply for an assessment of the costs may ask, within 30 days after the date on which the legal costs become payable. A third party payer liable for only part of the costs may ask only about that part.
GIVEN `lump sum bill` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the bill you were given is a lump sum bill (one total, not itemised)
      `day costs became payable` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the legal costs became payable (ISO date)
      `day I ask` IS A DATE @desc The day you make, or will make, the request for an itemised bill (ISO date)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Can I ask for an itemised bill` MEANS
    `s 187(1), (2) -- my request for an itemised bill is in time` `lump sum bill` `day costs became payable` `day I ask`

-- s 187(3) "The law practice must comply with the request within 21 days after the date on which the request is made".
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Party` `Act done`
`s 187(3) -- give the itemised bill within 21 days` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `my law practice`
    MUST `give the itemised bill`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `my law practice` BECAUSE "the itemised bill was not given within 21 days after the request (s 187(3))"

@export By when must my law practice give me the itemised bill I asked for (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 187(3))? Within 21 days after the date of a request made in time.
GIVEN `day I asked` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked for the itemised bill (ISO date)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I get my itemised bill` MEANS
    `the date` 21 `days after` `day I asked`

@export Did my law practice give me the itemised bill I asked for in time (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 187(3))? It must comply within 21 days after the date of the request.
GIVEN `day I asked` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked for the itemised bill (ISO date)
      `day I got it` IS A DATE @desc The day the law practice gave you the itemised bill (ISO date)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Was my itemised bill given in time` MEANS
    `done in time under` (`s 187(3) -- give the itemised bill within 21 days` 21) `by` `my law practice` `doing` `give the itemised bill` `at` (`the days from` `day I asked` `to` `day I got it`)

-- s 191 "A law practice must not make a charge for preparing or giving a bill, and any charge made for that purpose is
-- not recoverable by the law practice."
@export How much can my law practice charge me for preparing or giving me a bill (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 191), in dollars? Nothing: it must not charge for a bill, and any such charge cannot be recovered from you.
GIVEN `amount charged for the bill` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount, in dollars, the law practice charged you for preparing or giving the bill (0 if none)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much can I be charged for a bill` MEANS 0

-- s 190(1) "A law practice must give a client, on reasonable request, without charge and within a reasonable period, a
-- written report of the legal costs incurred". (2), (3): a practice retained by another practice reports through it,
-- unless the other practice has ceased to act. "on reasonable request" is an "if": without one, JUST FALSE in effect.
DECLARE `Progress report` IS ONE OF
    `yes: this law practice must give me, free and within a reasonable period, a written report of my legal costs to date or since the last bill (s 190(1))`
    `yes, through the law practice that retained it for me: that practice must give me the report, and this one must give it the information (s 190(2))`
    `no: s 190 requires a report only on a reasonable request`

GIVEN `requested` IS A BOOLEAN
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `retained by another practice that still acts` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Progress report`
`s 190 -- the progress report` `requested` `reasonable` `retained by another practice that still acts` MEANS
    IF NOT (`requested` AND `reasonable`)
    THEN `no: s 190 requires a report only on a reasonable request`
    ELSE IF `retained by another practice that still acts`
         THEN `yes, through the law practice that retained it for me: that practice must give me the report, and this one must give it the information (s 190(2))`
         ELSE `yes: this law practice must give me, free and within a reasonable period, a written report of my legal costs to date or since the last bill (s 190(1))`

@export Must my law practice give me a free written report of the legal costs I have run up so far (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 190)? Yes, on a reasonable request, without charge and within a reasonable period.
GIVEN `kind of client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client you are: an individual or other client not listed in s 170(2); or one of the commercial or government kinds
      `day first instructed` IS A DATE @desc The day you first instructed the law practice in this matter (ISO date)
      `I asked` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked the law practice for a report of your costs
      `my request was reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your request was reasonable (for example not repeated too often)
      `retained for me by another law practice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this law practice was retained for you by another law practice (for example a barrister briefed by your solicitor) that still acts for you
GIVETH A MAYBE `Progress report`
`Must I be given a progress report` MEANS
    IF `yes` (`s 170, Sch 4 cl 18 -- Part 4.3 applies to me` `kind of client` `day first instructed`)
    THEN JUST (`s 190 -- the progress report` `I asked` `my request was reasonable` `retained for me by another law practice`)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 195: interest on unpaid legal costs. (1) under the costs agreement's terms; (2) otherwise, if unpaid "30 days or more
-- after" the bill was given; (3) only if the bill states that interest is payable and its rate; (4) at a rate not above
-- the Uniform Rules rate -- General Rules r 75: the Reserve Bank's Cash Rate Target on the day the bill was issued plus 2
-- percentage points; (5) not on a bill given "more than 6 months after the completion of the matter", unless (6)(a) a lump
-- sum bill was given within the 6 months and the itemised bill was requested after them, or (6)(b) the bill was delayed or
-- withdrawn at my request. "must not" is a flat negative: JUST FALSE. When every condition is met: JUST TRUE.
DECLARE `Why the bill came late` IS ONE OF
    `the bill did not come more than 6 months after the matter was completed, or no other reason applies`
    `a lump sum bill came within the 6 months, and the itemised bill was requested after them (s 195(6)(a))`
    `the bill was not given within the 6 months, or was withdrawn, at my request (s 195(6)(b))`

GIVEN `the agreement deals with interest` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the bill` IS A DATE
      `the day interest starts` IS A DATE
      `the bill states interest and rate` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the rate charged` IS A NUMBER
      `the cash rate target` IS A NUMBER
      `the day completed` IS A DATE
      `why late` IS A `Why the bill came late`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 195 -- interest may be charged` `the agreement deals with interest` `the day of the bill` `the day interest starts` `the bill states interest and rate` `the rate charged` `the cash rate target` `the day completed` `why late` IF
        (`the agreement deals with interest` OR `the days from` `the day of the bill` `to` `the day interest starts` AT LEAST 30)
    AND `the bill states interest and rate`
    AND `the rate charged` AT MOST (`the cash rate target` PLUS 2)
    AND (    `the day` `the day of the bill` `is on or before` (`the same day` 6 `months after` `the day completed`)
         OR  (NOT (`why late` EQUALS `the bill did not come more than 6 months after the matter was completed, or no other reason applies`)))

@export May my law practice charge me interest on my unpaid legal costs (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 195, Uniform Rules r 75)? Only as the costs agreement allows or, if it is silent, once the costs are unpaid 30 days or more after the bill; only if the bill states that interest is payable and its rate; never above the Reserve Bank's Cash Rate Target on the bill's date plus 2 percentage points; and not on a bill given more than 6 months after the matter was completed, unless that delay was at your request or a lump sum bill came in time.
GIVEN `kind of client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client you are: an individual or other client not listed in s 170(2); or one of the commercial or government kinds
      `day first instructed` IS A DATE @desc The day you first instructed the law practice in this matter (ISO date)
      `costs agreement deals with interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your costs agreement deals with charging interest on unpaid costs
      `day of the bill` IS A DATE @desc The day the law practice gave you the bill (ISO date)
      `day interest starts` IS A DATE @desc The first day for which interest is charged (ISO date)
      `bill states interest and rate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the bill says that interest is payable and states the rate
      `rate charged` IS A NUMBER @desc The interest rate charged, in percent a year (e.g. 6.35)
      `cash rate target` IS A NUMBER @desc The Reserve Bank of Australia's Cash Rate Target on the day the bill was issued, in percent a year (e.g. 4.35)
      `day matter completed` IS A DATE @desc The day the matter was completed (ISO date)
      `why the bill came late` IS A `Why the bill came late` @desc If the bill came more than 6 months after the matter was completed, why: a lump sum bill came within the 6 months and you asked for the itemised bill later; or the bill was delayed or withdrawn at your request; otherwise the first choice
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May my lawyer charge me interest` MEANS
    IF `yes` (`s 170, Sch 4 cl 18 -- Part 4.3 applies to me` `kind of client` `day first instructed`)
    THEN JUST (`s 195 -- interest may be charged` `costs agreement deals with interest` `day of the bill` `day interest starts` `bill states interest and rate` `rate charged` `cash rate target` `day matter completed` `why the bill came late`)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `When my law practice may sue me for its costs (ss 178(1)(c), 194, 198(7)(b))`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 194(1) no proceedings "unless a bill has been given for the legal costs and the bill complies with the requirements
-- of this Law and the Uniform Rules"; (2) not "until (a) where the legal costs are the subject of a costs dispute before
-- the designated local regulatory authority--the authority has closed or resolved the dispute; and (b) at least 30 days
-- after the later of (i) the date on which the person is given the bill; or (ii) the date on which the person receives an
-- itemised bill following a request made in accordance with section 187". s 178(1)(c) after a disclosure failure, not
-- until assessed or determined. s 198(7)(b) not "until the costs assessment has been completed".
-- An "until" bar (ruling (d) of 2026-11-05): JUST TRUE while a bar holds, JUST FALSE once none does -- "Part 4.3 no
-- longer bars it", never "the practice may sue" (other law may still bar it). Fork F6: "at least 30 days after" a day is
-- reached on that day plus 30.
GIVEN `a complying bill given` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the bill` IS A DATE
      `itemised bill requested in time` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day itemised bill received` IS A DATE
      `costs dispute open` IS A BOOLEAN
      `disclosure failure not yet assessed or determined` IS A BOOLEAN
      `assessment applied for and not completed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `ss 178(1)(c), 194, 198(7)(b) -- I cannot yet be sued for the costs` `a complying bill given` `the day of the bill` `itemised bill requested in time` `the day itemised bill received` `costs dispute open` `disclosure failure not yet assessed or determined` `assessment applied for and not completed` `today` IF
        (NOT `a complying bill given`)
    OR  `costs dispute open`
    OR  `disclosure failure not yet assessed or determined`
    OR  `assessment applied for and not completed`
    OR  `the days from` `the later day` `to` `today` LESS THAN 30
    WHERE
        `the later day` MEANS
            IF `itemised bill requested in time`
            THEN `the later of` `the day of the bill` `and` `the day itemised bill received`
            ELSE `the day of the bill`

@export Is my law practice still barred by Part 4.3 from suing me for its legal costs (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) ss 178(1)(c), 194, 198(7)(b))? It cannot sue without a bill that complies with the Law, while a costs dispute about the costs is open before the Victorian Legal Services Commissioner, while a costs assessment you applied for is not completed, after a disclosure failure until the costs are assessed or the dispute determined, or until at least 30 days after the later of the day you were given the bill and the day you received an itemised bill you asked for in time. "No" means only that these sections no longer stand in its way.
GIVEN `kind of client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client you are (or would be, if you are a third party payer): an individual or other client not listed in s 170(2); or one of the commercial or government kinds
      `day first instructed` IS A DATE @desc The day the client first instructed the law practice in this matter (ISO date)
      `complying bill given` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been given a bill for the costs that complies with the Uniform Law and Uniform Rules (signed or naming a responsible principal, with a statement of your rights to dispute it and the time limits)
      `day of the bill` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given the bill (ISO date)
      `asked for itemised bill in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked for an itemised bill within 30 days after the costs became payable
      `day itemised bill received` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the itemised bill you asked for (ISO date); if not yet received, a day far in the future; if not asked, any day
      `costs dispute still open` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a costs dispute about these costs is before the Victorian Legal Services Commissioner and not yet closed or resolved
      `disclosure failure not yet resolved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice failed to make a required costs disclosure and the costs have not yet been assessed nor a costs dispute about them determined
      `assessment not completed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have applied for a costs assessment of these costs that has not yet been completed
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The day the law practice would start the proceedings (ISO date)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my lawyer barred from suing me for the costs` MEANS
    IF `yes` (`s 170, Sch 4 cl 18 -- Part 4.3 applies to me` `kind of client` `day first instructed`)
    THEN JUST (`ss 178(1)(c), 194, 198(7)(b) -- I cannot yet be sued for the costs` `complying bill given` `day of the bill` `asked for itemised bill in time` `day itemised bill received` `costs dispute still open` `disclosure failure not yet resolved` `assessment not completed` `today`)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Costs assessment (ss 196-205, Vic Act ss 97, 99(9))`
-- ===================================================================================================================

DECLARE `Who I am` IS ONE OF
    `the client`
    `a third party payer who owes the costs to the law practice`
    `a third party payer who owes the costs to the client or someone else`

DECLARE `Earlier costs dispute` IS ONE OF
    `there has been no costs dispute about these costs`
    `the Commissioner could not resolve the costs dispute and told the parties they may apply for a costs assessment`
    `the Commissioner arranged a costs assessment under s 284`
    `the costs dispute was resolved, determined by the Commissioner, or is still being dealt with`
    `VCAT determined the costs dispute under the Victorian Act s 99`

DECLARE `Whether I can have my costs assessed` IS ONE OF
    `yes: my application is within 12 months (s 198(1), (3))`
    `yes, though late: the Supreme Court has determined that it is just and fair for it to be dealt with (s 198(4))`
    `no: it is more than 12 months late and the Supreme Court has not determined that it is just and fair to deal with it (s 198(3), (4))`
    `no: the costs are or were the subject of a costs dispute before the Commissioner that was not left unresolved with notice of my right to an assessment (s 197)`
    `no: VCAT determined the costs dispute (Victorian Act s 99(9))`
    `the Commissioner's own assessment: the costs are being assessed under s 284, outside any time limit (ss 197(b), 284(2))`
    `no: Division 7 applies only to costs payable to my own law practice on a solicitor-client basis, not costs ordered to be paid to another party (s 196)`
    `not covered: Part 4.3 does not protect me (s 170, Sch 4 cl 18)`

-- s 198(3) "within 12 months after (a) the bill was given to, or the request for payment was made to, the client, third
-- party payer or other law practice; or (b) the legal costs were paid if neither a bill nor a request was made". The last
-- day is the same day 12 months later (fork F1). (4) the designated tribunal for s 198 is the Supreme Court (Vic Act s 10,
-- Table 2 item 2). (5) does not reach anyone s 170(1)(b) already takes out of the Part (finding 3).
GIVEN `solicitor-client` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the dispute` IS AN `Earlier costs dispute`
      `the day time runs from` IS A DATE
      `the day of application` IS A DATE
      `the Supreme Court found it just and fair` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Whether I can have my costs assessed`
`ss 196-198, Vic s 99(9) -- the costs assessment` `solicitor-client` `the dispute` `the day time runs from` `the day of application` `the Supreme Court found it just and fair` MEANS
    IF NOT `solicitor-client`
    THEN `no: Division 7 applies only to costs payable to my own law practice on a solicitor-client basis, not costs ordered to be paid to another party (s 196)`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the dispute`
         WHEN `the costs dispute was resolved, determined by the Commissioner, or is still being dealt with` THEN `no: the costs are or were the subject of a costs dispute before the Commissioner that was not left unresolved with notice of my right to an assessment (s 197)`
         WHEN `VCAT determined the costs dispute under the Victorian Act s 99` THEN `no: VCAT determined the costs dispute (Victorian Act s 99(9))`
         WHEN `the Commissioner arranged a costs assessment under s 284` THEN `the Commissioner's own assessment: the costs are being assessed under s 284, outside any time limit (ss 197(b), 284(2))`
         OTHERWISE
             IF `the day` `the day of application` `is on or before` (`the same day` 12 `months after` `the day time runs from`)
             THEN `yes: my application is within 12 months (s 198(1), (3))`
             ELSE IF `the Supreme Court found it just and fair`
                  THEN `yes, though late: the Supreme Court has determined that it is just and fair for it to be dealt with (s 198(4))`
                  ELSE `no: it is more than 12 months late and the Supreme Court has not determined that it is just and fair to deal with it (s 198(3), (4))`

@export Can I apply to have my law practice's legal costs assessed by a costs assessor (the Costs Court in Victoria), and is my application in time (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) ss 196-198, Victorian Act ss 97, 99(9))? A client, or a third party payer, who has paid or must pay the costs may apply within 12 months after the bill or request for payment (or after paying, if there was neither); later only if the Supreme Court determines it is just and fair. Costs that went to a costs dispute before the Commissioner can be assessed only if the Commissioner could not resolve it and said so, and not after VCAT has determined it.
GIVEN `kind of client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client you are (or would be, if you are a third party payer): an individual or other client not listed in s 170(2); or one of the commercial or government kinds
      `day first instructed` IS A DATE @desc The day the client first instructed the law practice in this matter (ISO date)
      `who I am` IS A `Who I am` @desc Whether you are the client, a third party payer who owes the costs to the law practice, or a third party payer who owes them to the client or someone else (for example an insurer)
      `costs owed to my own lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether these are costs payable to the law practice for its services (solicitor-client costs), not costs a court or tribunal ordered you to pay another party
      `earlier costs dispute` IS AN `Earlier costs dispute` @desc What became of any costs dispute about these costs before the Victorian Legal Services Commissioner: none; the Commissioner could not resolve it and told you of your right to apply for assessment; the Commissioner arranged an assessment; it was resolved, determined or is still going; or VCAT determined it
      `day bill or request given` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given the bill or the request for payment; if there was neither, the day you paid the costs (ISO date)
      `day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply, or will apply, for the assessment (ISO date)
      `Supreme Court found late application just` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the application is more than 12 months late, whether the Supreme Court has determined, having regard to the delay and the reasons for it, that it is just and fair to deal with it; otherwise answer no
GIVETH A `Whether I can have my costs assessed`
`Can I have my costs assessed` MEANS
    IF `yes` (`s 170, Sch 4 cl 18 -- Part 4.3 applies to me` `kind of client` `day first instructed`)
    THEN `ss 196-198, Vic s 99(9) -- the costs assessment` `costs owed to my own lawyer` `earlier costs dispute` `day bill or request given` `day I apply` `Supreme Court found late application just`
    ELSE `not covered: Part 4.3 does not protect me (s 170, Sch 4 cl 18)`

@export What is the last day on which I can apply for a costs assessment of my law practice's bill without needing the Supreme Court's leave (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 198(3))? 12 months after the bill or request for payment was given to you, or, if there was neither, after the costs were paid.
GIVEN `day bill or request given` IS A DATE @desc The day you were given the bill or the request for payment; if there was neither, the day you paid the costs (ISO date)
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the last day to apply for a costs assessment` MEANS
    `the same day` 12 `months after` `day bill or request given`

-- s 204(2) "Unless the costs assessor believes that in all the circumstances it is not fair and reasonable for the costs to
-- be paid otherwise, the costs of a costs assessment are payable by a law practice if (a) [failure to disclose] (b)
-- [failure to disclose in the manner required] (c) the law practice's costs have been reduced by 15% or more on
-- assessment." Otherwise (1): the costs assessor determines them.
DECLARE `Who pays for the assessment` IS ONE OF
    `my law practice pays the costs of the costs assessment (s 204(2))`
    `the costs assessor decides who pays the costs of the costs assessment (s 204(1))`

GIVEN `claimed` IS A NUMBER
      `assessed` IS A NUMBER
      `failed to disclose` IS A BOOLEAN
      `assessor finds otherwise fair` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Who pays for the assessment`
`s 204 -- who pays for the assessment` `claimed` `assessed` `failed to disclose` `assessor finds otherwise fair` MEANS
    IF (    `failed to disclose`
         OR (`claimed` GREATER THAN 0 AND (`claimed` MINUS `assessed`) TIMES 100 AT LEAST `claimed` TIMES 15))
       AND (NOT `assessor finds otherwise fair`)
    THEN `my law practice pays the costs of the costs assessment (s 204(2))`
    ELSE `the costs assessor decides who pays the costs of the costs assessment (s 204(1))`

@export Who pays for the costs assessment of my law practice's bill (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 204)? The law practice pays if it failed to make a required costs disclosure (or made it in the wrong way) or its costs are cut by 15% or more, unless the costs assessor believes that would not be fair and reasonable; otherwise the costs assessor decides.
GIVEN `costs claimed` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs the law practice claimed (the amount assessed), in dollars
      `costs as assessed` IS A NUMBER @desc The legal costs the costs assessor found payable, in dollars
      `failed to disclose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the law practice failed to disclose something Part 4.3 Division 3 required, or failed to disclose it in the way required
      `assessor believes it unfair` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the costs assessor believes it is not fair and reasonable in all the circumstances for the law practice to pay the costs of the assessment; answer no if not so decided
GIVETH A `Who pays for the assessment`
`Who pays for my costs assessment` MEANS
    `s 204 -- who pays for the assessment` `costs claimed` `costs as assessed` `failed to disclose` `assessor believes it unfair`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `Costs disputes with the Victorian Legal Services Commissioner and at VCAT (ss 268, 272, 291-294, Vic Act s 99)`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 272(3) the required period: "(a) 60 days after the legal costs become payable, except as provided by paragraph (b);
-- or (b) if an itemised bill was requested in respect of those costs in accordance with section 187(2)--30 days after
-- the request was complied with." (2) waiver if "(a) the complaint is made within 4 months after the required period;
-- and (b) it is just and fair ...; and (c) the lawyer or law practice has not commenced legal proceedings in respect of
-- the legal costs". The Chapter 5 authority is the Victorian Legal Services Commissioner (Vic Act s 10, Table 1 item 7).
GIVEN `the day payable` IS A DATE
      `itemised requested in time` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day request complied with` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 272(3) -- the last day of the required period` `the day payable` `itemised requested in time` `the day request complied with` MEANS
    IF `itemised requested in time`
    THEN `the date` 30 `days after` `the day request complied with`
    ELSE `the date` 60 `days after` `the day payable`

@export What is the last day on which I can complain to the Victorian Legal Services Commissioner about my legal costs as a costs dispute (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) s 272(2), (3))? 60 days after the costs became payable; or, if you asked for an itemised bill in time, 30 days after the law practice gave it to you.
GIVEN `day costs became payable` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the legal costs became payable (ISO date)
      `asked for itemised bill in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked for an itemised bill within 30 days after the costs became payable
      `day itemised bill given` IS A DATE @desc The day the law practice complied with your request by giving the itemised bill (ISO date); any day if you did not ask
GIVETH A DATE
`What is the last day to complain about my costs` MEANS
    `s 272(3) -- the last day of the required period` `day costs became payable` `asked for itemised bill in time` `day itemised bill given`

DECLARE `Whether my costs complaint is in time` IS ONE OF
    `in time: made within the required period (s 272(2), (3))`
    `late, but the time limit can be and has been waived: within 4 months after the required period, the Commissioner is satisfied it is just and fair, and the law practice has not started proceedings for the costs (s 272(2))`
    `late, and the time limit can still be waived if the Commissioner is satisfied that it is just and fair to deal with it (s 272(2))`
    `too late for a costs dispute: the time limit cannot be waived (s 272(2)); the conduct may still be complained of as a disciplinary matter within 3 years (s 272(1))`
    `no relief as a costs dispute: a commercial or government client cannot obtain relief on a consumer matter (s 268(3))`

GIVEN `the last day` IS A DATE
      `the day of complaint` IS A DATE
      `proceedings started` IS A BOOLEAN
      `Commissioner decided` IS A BOOLEAN
      `Commissioner satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Whether my costs complaint is in time`
`s 272(2) -- the costs complaint in time` `the last day` `the day of complaint` `proceedings started` `Commissioner decided` `Commissioner satisfied` MEANS
    IF `the day` `the day of complaint` `is on or before` `the last day`
    THEN `in time: made within the required period (s 272(2), (3))`
    ELSE IF     `the day` `the day of complaint` `is on or before` (`the same day` 4 `months after` `the last day`)
            AND (NOT `proceedings started`)
            AND (NOT (`Commissioner decided` AND (NOT `Commissioner satisfied`)))
         THEN IF `Commissioner decided`
              THEN `late, but the time limit can be and has been waived: within 4 months after the required period, the Commissioner is satisfied it is just and fair, and the law practice has not started proceedings for the costs (s 272(2))`
              ELSE `late, and the time limit can still be waived if the Commissioner is satisfied that it is just and fair to deal with it (s 272(2))`
         ELSE `too late for a costs dispute: the time limit cannot be waived (s 272(2)); the conduct may still be complained of as a disciplinary matter within 3 years (s 272(1))`

@export Is my complaint about my legal costs to the Victorian Legal Services Commissioner in time to be dealt with as a costs dispute (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) ss 268(3), 272(2), (3))? It must be made within 60 days after the costs became payable (or 30 days after an itemised bill you asked for in time); up to 4 months later the Commissioner may waive the limit if satisfied it is just and fair and the law practice has not started proceedings for the costs.
GIVEN `kind of client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client you are: an individual or other client not listed in s 170(2); or one of the commercial or government kinds
      `day costs became payable` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the legal costs became payable (ISO date)
      `asked for itemised bill in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked for an itemised bill within 30 days after the costs became payable
      `day itemised bill given` IS A DATE @desc The day the law practice complied with your request by giving the itemised bill (ISO date); any day if you did not ask
      `day of complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day you make, or made, the complaint (ISO date)
      `lawyer has sued for the costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the lawyer or law practice has started legal proceedings for these costs
      `Commissioner has decided on waiver` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner has decided whether to waive the time limit; answer no if not yet
      `Commissioner satisfied it is just and fair` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If the Commissioner has decided, whether the Commissioner was satisfied, having regard to the delay and its reasons, that it is just and fair to deal with the complaint
GIVETH A `Whether my costs complaint is in time`
`Is my costs complaint in time` MEANS
    IF `s 170(2) -- a commercial or government client` `kind of client`
    THEN `no relief as a costs dispute: a commercial or government client cannot obtain relief on a consumer matter (s 268(3))`
    ELSE `s 272(2) -- the costs complaint in time` (`s 272(3) -- the last day of the required period` `day costs became payable` `asked for itemised bill in time` `day itemised bill given`) `day of complaint` `lawyer has sued for the costs` `Commissioner has decided on waiver` `Commissioner satisfied it is just and fair`

-- s 291(1): the Commissioner deals with a costs dispute as a consumer matter "if (a) the total bill for legal costs is
-- less than $100 000 (indexed) payable in respect of any one matter; or (b) the total bill ... equals or is more than
-- $100 000 (indexed) ..., but the total amount in dispute is less than $10 000 (indexed)". (2) otherwise the Commissioner
-- is not to deal with it, and informs the parties of their right to a costs assessment or to apply to VCAT (Vic Act s 99).
-- s 294: GST is disregarded. The Law's figures are used: no indexed figure is deposited.
`the s 291 bill limit` MEANS 100000
`the s 291-293 dispute limit` MEANS 10000
`the VCAT limit in the Victorian Act s 99` MEANS 25000

DECLARE `Whether the Commissioner can deal with my costs dispute` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the total bill is less than $100 000 (s 291(1)(a))`
    `yes: the total bill is $100 000 or more, but less than $10 000 is in dispute (s 291(1)(b))`
    `no: the Commissioner must instead tell me of my right to a costs assessment or to apply to VCAT (s 291(2))`
    `no relief as a costs dispute: a commercial or government client cannot obtain relief on a consumer matter (s 268(3))`

GIVEN `the total bill` IS A NUMBER
      `the amount in dispute` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Whether the Commissioner can deal with my costs dispute`
`s 291 -- the Commissioner deals with the costs dispute` `the total bill` `the amount in dispute` MEANS
    IF `the total bill` LESS THAN `the s 291 bill limit`
    THEN `yes: the total bill is less than $100 000 (s 291(1)(a))`
    ELSE IF `the amount in dispute` LESS THAN `the s 291-293 dispute limit`
         THEN `yes: the total bill is $100 000 or more, but less than $10 000 is in dispute (s 291(1)(b))`
         ELSE `no: the Commissioner must instead tell me of my right to a costs assessment or to apply to VCAT (s 291(2))`

@export Can the Victorian Legal Services Commissioner deal with my dispute about my lawyer's bill (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) ss 268(3), 291, 294)? Yes if the total bill for the matter is under $100 000, or if it is more but under $10 000 is in dispute (GST left out of both); otherwise the Commissioner tells you of your right to a costs assessment or to apply to VCAT.
GIVEN `kind of client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client you are: an individual or other client not listed in s 170(2); or one of the commercial or government kinds
      `total bill` IS A NUMBER @desc The total bill for legal costs in the matter, in dollars, excluding GST
      `amount in dispute` IS A NUMBER @desc The total amount of the legal costs you dispute, in dollars, excluding GST
GIVETH A `Whether the Commissioner can deal with my costs dispute`
`Can the Commissioner deal with my costs dispute` MEANS
    IF `s 170(2) -- a commercial or government client` `kind of client`
    THEN `no relief as a costs dispute: a commercial or government client cannot obtain relief on a consumer matter (s 268(3))`
    ELSE `s 291 -- the Commissioner deals with the costs dispute` `total bill` `amount in dispute`

-- s 292(2) a binding determination may be made where (a) the Commissioner is unable to resolve a s 291(1) dispute and
-- (b) "the total amount of legal costs still in dispute is less than $10 000 (indexed)"; (3) the amount ordered must be
-- less than $10 000. s 293(2): unresolved and (i) $10 000 or more still in dispute, or (ii) less but the Commissioner
-- notifies in writing that it is unable to resolve it: the Commissioner ceases and informs the parties of their rights.
DECLARE `What happens to my unresolved costs dispute` IS ONE OF
    `the Commissioner may make a binding determination of the amount payable, less than $10 000 (s 292); or may notify in writing that it cannot resolve the dispute, and then must tell me of my right to a costs assessment or to VCAT (s 293(2)(b)(ii))`
    `the Commissioner has notified that it cannot resolve the dispute: it must stop and tell me of my right to a costs assessment or to apply to VCAT (s 293(2)(b)(ii))`
    `the Commissioner must stop and tell me of my right to a costs assessment or to apply to VCAT: $10 000 or more is still in dispute (s 293(2)(b)(i))`
    `not covered: the Commissioner cannot deal with this costs dispute (ss 268(3), 291)`

GIVEN `can deal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `still in dispute` IS A NUMBER
      `notified unable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `What happens to my unresolved costs dispute`
`ss 292, 293 -- the unresolved costs dispute` `can deal` `still in dispute` `notified unable` MEANS
    IF NOT `can deal`
    THEN `not covered: the Commissioner cannot deal with this costs dispute (ss 268(3), 291)`
    ELSE IF `still in dispute` AT LEAST `the s 291-293 dispute limit`
         THEN `the Commissioner must stop and tell me of my right to a costs assessment or to apply to VCAT: $10 000 or more is still in dispute (s 293(2)(b)(i))`
         ELSE IF `notified unable`
              THEN `the Commissioner has notified that it cannot resolve the dispute: it must stop and tell me of my right to a costs assessment or to apply to VCAT (s 293(2)(b)(ii))`
              ELSE `the Commissioner may make a binding determination of the amount payable, less than $10 000 (s 292); or may notify in writing that it cannot resolve the dispute, and then must tell me of my right to a costs assessment or to VCAT (s 293(2)(b)(ii))`

@export The Victorian Legal Services Commissioner could not resolve my costs dispute: what happens next (Legal Profession Uniform Law (Victoria) ss 292-294)? With under $10 000 still in dispute (excluding GST) the Commissioner may make a binding determination of the amount payable, or notify that it cannot resolve the dispute; with $10 000 or more, or after such a notice, the Commissioner stops and tells you of your right to a costs assessment or to apply to VCAT.
GIVEN `kind of client` IS A `Kind of client` @desc What kind of client you are: an individual or other client not listed in s 170(2); or one of the commercial or government kinds
      `total bill` IS A NUMBER @desc The total bill for legal costs in the matter, in dollars, excluding GST
      `amount first in dispute` IS A NUMBER @desc The total amount of legal costs you disputed when you complained, in dollars, excluding GST
      `amount still in dispute` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of legal costs still in dispute after the Commissioner's attempt to resolve it, in dollars, excluding GST
      `Commissioner notified it cannot resolve it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner has notified you in writing that it is unable to resolve the dispute
GIVETH A `What happens to my unresolved costs dispute`
`What happens to my unresolved costs dispute` MEANS
    `ss 292, 293 -- the unresolved costs dispute` `the Commissioner can deal with it` `amount still in dispute` `Commissioner notified it cannot resolve it`
    WHERE
        `the Commissioner can deal with it` MEANS
                (NOT (`s 170(2) -- a commercial or government client` `kind of client`))
            AND (NOT (`s 291 -- the Commissioner deals with the costs dispute` `total bill` `amount first in dispute` EQUALS `no: the Commissioner must instead tell me of my right to a costs assessment or to apply to VCAT (s 291(2))`))

-- Victorian Act s 99(1) "A party to a complaint that involves a costs dispute may apply to VCAT to determine the costs
-- dispute if (a) the total amount of legal costs in dispute is not more than $25 000 (indexed); and (b) the parties have
-- been informed by the Victorian Commissioner under section 291(2) or 293(1) ... of their right to apply to VCAT". (2)
-- "within 60 days after the Victorian Commissioner informs the parties". (4)(a) VCAT's order cannot exceed $25 000.
-- Finding 4: s 99 has no GST rule like s 294; the amount is taken as the person states it (fork F7).
DECLARE `Whether I can take my costs dispute to VCAT` IS ONE OF
    `yes: I can apply to VCAT, which can order an amount payable of up to $25 000 (Victorian Act s 99(1), (2), (4))`
    `no: more than $25 000 is in dispute (Victorian Act s 99(1)(a))`
    `no: the Commissioner has not informed me of my right to apply to VCAT under s 291(2) or s 293(1) (Victorian Act s 99(1)(b))`
    `no: my application is not within 60 days after the Commissioner informed me (Victorian Act s 99(2))`

GIVEN `in dispute` IS A NUMBER
      `informed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day informed` IS A DATE
      `the day of application` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Whether I can take my costs dispute to VCAT`
`Vic s 99 -- the costs dispute at VCAT` `in dispute` `informed` `the day informed` `the day of application` MEANS
    IF `in dispute` GREATER THAN `the VCAT limit in the Victorian Act s 99`
    THEN `no: more than $25 000 is in dispute (Victorian Act s 99(1)(a))`
    ELSE IF NOT `informed`
         THEN `no: the Commissioner has not informed me of my right to apply to VCAT under s 291(2) or s 293(1) (Victorian Act s 99(1)(b))`
         ELSE IF `the day` `the day of application` `is on or before` (`the date` 60 `days after` `the day informed`)
              THEN `yes: I can apply to VCAT, which can order an amount payable of up to $25 000 (Victorian Act s 99(1), (2), (4))`
              ELSE `no: my application is not within 60 days after the Commissioner informed me (Victorian Act s 99(2))`

@export Can I apply to VCAT to determine my dispute about my lawyer's bill, and is my application in time (Legal Profession Uniform Law Application Act 2014 (Vic) s 99)? A party to a costs dispute complaint may apply if no more than $25 000 is in dispute and the Victorian Legal Services Commissioner has told the parties of their right to apply (because the dispute was too large for it, or it could not resolve it), within 60 days after being told. VCAT can order an amount payable of up to $25 000, including nil.
GIVEN `amount in dispute` IS A NUMBER @desc The total amount of legal costs in dispute, in dollars
      `Commissioner told me I may apply to VCAT` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner has informed the parties, under s 291(2) or s 293(1) of the Uniform Law, of their right to apply to VCAT
      `day I was told` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commissioner informed the parties of that right (ISO date)
      `day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you apply, or will apply, to VCAT (ISO date)
GIVETH A `Whether I can take my costs dispute to VCAT`
`Can I take my costs dispute to VCAT` MEANS
    `Vic s 99 -- the costs dispute at VCAT` `amount in dispute` `Commissioner told me I may apply to VCAT` `day I was told` `day I apply`
